# `declaration:rows` (tflw `G10`, `D1384`, SPEC §4.3). Three rows, each asking `arrival-server.mjs`
# for `/after/<ms>` once: `/after/0` answers 200 at once and `/after/600000` answers 503 for ten
# minutes, so exactly one row's last response is a 200 and two are 503s. Each row passes on its own —
# neither status is an error to it — so the counts are visible only to the `rows` block, which is the
# one place that sees every row's answer. Its twin `rows-miscounted.tflw` asks for the wrong counts.

with each
  | ms     |
  | 0      |
  | 600000 |
  | 600000 |
test "row {ms} asks once"
  api GET /after/{ms}
  expect status is less than 600
rows
  expect exactly 1 row status equals 200
  expect 2 rows status equals 503
  expect no rows status equals 500
